  "question": "Which word surfaces does comparing denylist entries and passage tokens as STEMS newly reach, beyond what the literal `entry` / `entry + s` tests already reached?",
  "ruling": "These, and this file is the vocabulary the answer is exhaustive OVER. The v2.6.1 release report published this census twice as 'enumerated over all sixteen entries' and it was wrong both times: the first version named `pays`, which does not match, and omitted six surfaces; the second omitted `carefulness` and `medicalization`, which Sol's verification review then found. Both failures were the same step — a candidate space generated mechanically and then filtered to real English BY HAND, where a hand that forgets a word leaves no trace. So the hand judgments are now the fixture. `tests/lab-match-behavior.test.mjs` regenerates the mechanical space from the stemmer's own suffix inventory and requires every candidate in it to carry an explicit verdict here, which is what makes an omission impossible rather than merely unlikely.",
  "notExhaustiveOver": "English. A surface is only in this file if the stemmer's suffix rules generate it from an entry or an entry's stem, so an irregular form reaching the same stem by another route would not appear — `paid` is the known example and it does not match anyway. Claims of exhaustiveness are about the generated space, never about the language.",
  "wordStandard": "Listed in a standard English dictionary, or in settled technical use in the register these entries come from. Marginal calls are recorded as verdicts rather than dropped silently, so a reviewer can contest one word instead of the whole table: `hosters` and `payed` are the two the maintainer considers arguable and both are counted as words, which is the direction that makes the census wider and the cost larger.",
  "generator": {
